ftmenu"><a href="/django-model">Django Model</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-view">Django View</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-template">Django Template</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-url-mapping">URL Mapping</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-static-files-handling">Static Files Handling</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-modelforms">ModelForms</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-forms">Django Forms</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-form-validation">Form Validation</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-file-upload">File Upload</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-database-connectitvity">Database Connectitvity</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-database-migrations">Database Migrations</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-middleware">Django Middleware</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-request-and-response">Request and Response</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-exceptions">Django Exceptions</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-session">Django Session</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-cookie">Django Cookie</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-csv-output">Djnago CSV Output</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-pdf-output">Django PDF Output</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-and-bootstrap">Django and Bootstrap</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-deploy-on-github">Deploy on Github</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-mail-setup">Django Mail Setup</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-default-crud">Django Default CRUD</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-crud-application">CRUD Application</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-redirects">Django Redirects</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-on_delete">Django on_delete</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-class-based-generic-views">Django Class Based Generic Views</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-usercreationform">Django UserCreationForm</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-image-upload">Django Image Upload</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-orm-queries">Django ORM Queries</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-form-widget">Django Form Widget</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/ruby-on-rails-vs-django">Ruby on Rails vs. Django</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/select_related-and-prefetch_related-in-django">select_related and prefetch_related</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-custom-user-model">Django Custom User Model</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-user-registration-with-email-confirmation">Django User Registration with Email Confirmation</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-to-use-django-widget-tweaks">How to use Django Widget Tweaks</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-shortcuts">Django Shortcuts</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-to-connect-mysql-to-django">How to connect MySQL to Django</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-to-use-f-expression">How to use F() Expression</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-news-app">Django News App</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/steps-to-improve-stability-in-the-django-app-on-heroku">Steps to Improve Stability in the Django App on Heroku</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/todo-webapp-using-django">ToDo Webapp using Django</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/fixtures-in-django">Fixtures in Django</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/dokku-django">Dokku Django</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/modelSerializer-in-serializers-django-rest-framework">ModelSerializer in serializers - Django REST Framework</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/relational-fields-in-django-models">Relational Fields in Django Models</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/url-fields-in-serializers-django-rest-framework">URL Fields in Serializers - Django REST Framework</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading1"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse1" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse1" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">Misc.</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading1" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse1"><div class="accordion-body"><ul><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-cms-vs-wordpress">Django CMS vs Wordpress</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-vs-laravel">Django vs Laravel</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-vs-django">PHP vs. Django</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/college-management-system-using-django-python-project">College Management System using Django - Python Project</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/create-a-word-counter-app-using-django">Create a Word Counter App Using Django</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/django-project-to-create-a-comments-system">Django Project to Create a Comments System</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/voting-system-project-using-django-framework">Voting System Project Using Django Framework</a></li></ul></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="col-12 col-md-7"><div class="overview"><html><head></head><body><div id="city"><table><tbody><tr><td><div id="bottomnextup"><a class="next" href="create-a-word-counter-app-using-django">next →</a> <a class="next" href="php-vs-django">← prev</a></div><h1 class="h1">College Management System using Django - Python Project</h1><div style="font-size:14px;margin-top:5px;margin-bottom:5px;color:#6f6e6e"><span>18 May 2025</span> | <span><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 10 min read</span></div><p>In this article, we will make a step-by-step college management system using Django, which is a powerful framework in Python. This project helps administrators to effectively handle key aspects of college operations, like student records, teacher profiles, course management, and attendance tracking. You will learn how to set up and structure the Django project, implement the necessary features, and ensure the smooth management of important education data. At the end of this article, you will have a fully functional college Management System that you can easily add according to your requirements. Whether you are looking to develop a system for your institution or simply want to explore the capabilities of Django, this project helps you with a solid foundation for creating scalable and reliable web apps.</p><p>We will make this project step by step by following this table of contents:</p><ol class="points"><li>Introduction to Django.</li><li>Features of the college management system</li><li>Setting up the Django project</li><li>Creating Models</li><li>Building Views and Templates</li><li>URL Routing</li><li>Admin Interface</li><li>Testing the Application</li><li>Conclusion</li></ol><h2 class="h2">Introduction to Django:</h2><p>Django is a high-level, open-source web framework designed to facilitate the fast development of secure and maintainable web applications. Written in <a href="python-tutorial">Python</a>, it follows the <a href="django-mvt">Model-View-Template (MVT)</a> architecture, helping developers create scalable and structured web applications. One of the core principles of Django is the "Don't Repeat Yourself" (DRY) principle, which encourages the reusability and maintainability of code.</p><h3 class="h3">Key Feature of Django:</h3><p>Django has various built-in features that help build effective web applications:</p><ol class="points"><li><strong>Object-Relational Mapper (ORM):</strong> Django's ORM, therefore, interacts with databases from Python code but not SQL itself. This saves developers the labor of database management and allows integration with several types of database systems, including PostgreSQL, MySQL, and SQLite, among others.</li><li><strong>Authentication System:</strong> Django offers a robust authentication system that includes user login, logout, password hashing, and user permission. This removes the requirement of building an authentication mechanism from scratch.</li><li><strong>Admin Panel:</strong> One of Django's most powerful features is its admin panel, which is automatically generated by the admin interface. This panel allows admins to handle the application's data using a user-friendly dashboard, decreasing the requirement for manually building a separate admin panel.</li><li><strong>Security Feature:</strong> Django comes with built-in protection against common security vulnerabilities like SQL injection, cross-site scripting (XSS), cross-site request forgery (CSRF), and clickjacking, making it a secure choice for developing a web application.</li><li><strong>Scalability and Versatility:</strong> Django is used by websites like Instagram, Pinterest, and Mozilla, which proves its ability to handle high traffic and scale efficiently. Django is suitable for a wide range of applications, from simple websites to complex websites.</li><li><strong>Built-in URL Routing:</strong> Django offers easy URL mapping utilizing its built-in routing system, which makes it simple to define and handle URLs for different views in an application.</li><li><strong>Templating Engine:</strong> Django's powerful templating engine creates Dynamic HTML pages with reusable components, improving development efficiency and maintainability.</li><li><strong>Built-in Caching and Middleware:</strong> Django offers a caching mechanism to improve performance and a middle framework that allows developers to process requests and responses efficiently.</li></ol><p>These powerful features of Django make web development simple and enable developers to focus on writing logical applications rather than dealing with repetitive tasks. In this next part, we will see how to set up a Django project and build a functional College Management System web application.</p><h2 class="h2">Feature of The College Management System:</h2><p>The College Management System is designed to provide a structured and efficient way to handle various aspects of an educational institution. Here are some of the core features that this system will contain.</p><ol class="points"><li><strong>Student Management:</strong> Managing student records is an important part of any educational institution. This model will help administrators to:<ol class="pointsa"><li>Add new students with the details like name, date of birth, contact information, and admission number.</li><li>Update student information when needed, like changing the course enrollment, updating addresses, or modifying personal details.</li><li>Deleted student records when the student is passed out from the college or leaves the college.</li><li>View the student profile with complete academic and personal details.</li></ol></li><li><strong>Teacher Management:</strong> This model helps to manage the faculty members of the college efficiently. This feature consists of:<ol class="pointsa"><li>Adding new teachers with important details like name, qualification, specialization, and contact information.</li><li>Assign teacher to specific courses on the basis of their expertise.</li><li>Update the profiles of the teachers when needed, like changing the designation or department.</li><li>Manage the records of the teacher by keeping track of their employment status, subjects taught and class schedules.</li></ol></li><li><strong>Management of Course:</strong> This module offers an easy creation and management of courses with the following functionalities.<ol class="pointsa"><li>Add new courses, adding the name of the course, code, duration, and hours of credit.</li><li>Assigning students and teachers to courses that ensure proper enrollment and allocation of faculty.</li><li>Updating details of course like syllabus, prerequisites, and schedule.</li><li>Deleting or attaining courses that are no longer offered.</li></ol></li><li><strong>Management of Attendance:</strong> Tracking student attendance is important for the monitoring of academics. This module offers:<ol class="pointsa"><li>Recording of attendance of students on a daily or pre-class basis.</li><li>Generating reports of attendance to track student participation over the period.</li><li>Marking absences and reasons, like medical leave or official permissions.</li><li>Notifying students or guardians in case of irregular patterns of attendance.</li></ol></li><li><strong>Admin Panel:</strong> Django offers a power admin panel, which can be customized to manage all the system features. The admin panel contains:<ol class="pointsa"><li>Authentication and Permission of Users, which allows different access levels for administrators, teachers, and students.</li><li>Overview of Dashboard, which gives a summary of students, teachers, courses, and attendance statistics.</li><li>Management of Data, which enables administrators to view, edit and delete the records easily.</li><li>System settings offer institution-specific configuration customization.</li></ol></li></ol><h3 class="h3">Additional Features (Enhancements of Future):</h3><p>In future updates, the system can be expanded to contain:</p><ol class="points"><li>Examination and Grading System: Manage exams, assignments, and performance of student reports.</li><li>Management of Library: Keep track of the books that are issued, returns, and available resources.</li><li>Management of Fee: Manage fees for the student, invoices, and payment tracking.</li><li>Dashboard for student and teacher: Offers dashboards that are personalized with relevant data.</li><li>Notifications and Messaging: Offers communication between students, teachers and administrators.</li></ol><h3 class="h3">Setting Up the Django Project:</h3><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Install Django:</p><p>To get started with the project, first install the Django framework in your system using the pip command:</p><p><strong>Code:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">pip install Django
</textarea></div><p>Once Django is installed on the system, use the following command to create a new Django Project.</p><p><strong>Command:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">django-admin startproject college_management
cd college_management
</textarea></div><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Creating a Django App:</p><p>To move forward with creating the college management system, we need to first create an app called college by using the command:</p><p><strong>Command:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">python manage.py startapp college
</textarea></div><p>To register this app in your project, add the new app to the INSTALLED_APPS list in the setting.py file:</p><p><strong>Code:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code"># Application definition

INSTALLED_APPS = [
    'django.contrib.admin',
    'django.contrib.auth',
    'django.contrib.contenttypes',
    'django.contrib.sessions',
    'django.contrib.messages',
    'django.contrib.staticfiles',
    'college'
]
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Creating Models:</h3><p>Models define the format of the database. Let's create models for students, teachers, courses, and attendance.</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Define Models in models.py file in the college app.</p><p>Open college/models.py and add the following code:</p><p><strong>Code:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">from django.db import models

class Student(models.Model):
    name = models.CharField(max_length=100)
    roll_number = models.CharField(max_length=10, unique=True)
    email = models.EmailField(unique=True)
    date_of_birth = models.DateField()

    def __str__(self):
        return self.name

class Teacher(models.Model):
    name = models.CharField(max_length=100)
    email = models.EmailField(unique=True)
    department = models.CharField(max_length=50)

    def __str__(self):
        return self.name

class Course(models.Model):
    name = models.CharField(max_length=100)
    code = models.CharField(max_length=10, unique=True)
    teacher = models.ForeignKey(Teacher, on_delete=models.CASCADE)

    def __str__(self):
        return self.name

class Attendance(models.Model):
    student = models.ForeignKey(Student, on_delete=models.CASCADE)
    course = models.ForeignKey(Course, on_delete=models.CASCADE)
    date = models.DateField()
    status = models.BooleanField(default=False)  # True for present, False for absent

    def __str__(self):
        return f"{self.student.name} - {self.course.name} - {self.date}"
</textarea></div><p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p><p>This Django model is about the definition of a College Management System, having four major classes. The Student model has the following information about students: name, unique roll number, email, and date of birth. The Teacher model consists of the teacher's name, unique email, and department. The Course model is related to courses that are represented with the help of the following details: name, unique course code, and foreign key to the teacher to make sure each course has an assigned instructor. The Attendance model monitors student attendance. It refers both to the Student model and Course models. Both include a date field and a boolean status field. True signifies a case of presence, while False signifies the case of absence. For improved database management, in each model, the __str__ method has been defined for returning a meaningful string representation of the models. Finally, it includes the following: on_delete=models.CASCADE removes related records in case the reference object gets removed. This structure aids in the effective management of student records, faculty assignments, course enrollments, and attendance tracking.</p><p>After defining the models, migrate them into the database by applying migrations. To migrate the model, use the following command.</p><p><strrong>Command:</strrong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">python manage.py makemigrations
python manage.py migrate
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Building Views and Templates:</h3><p>In the counter/views.py file, create a view for handling the text input and count the words.</p><p><strong>Code:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">from django.shortcuts import render
from .models import Student, Teacher, Course

def student_list(request):
    students = Student.objects.all()
    return render(request, 'college/student_list.html', {'students': students})

def teacher_list(request):
    teachers = Teacher.objects.all()
    return render(request, 'college/teacher_list.html', {'teachers': teachers})

def course_list(request):
    courses = Course.objects.all()
    return render(request, 'college/course_list.html', {'courses': courses})
</textarea></div><p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p><p>The above code defines three functions to extract and display lists of students, teachers, and courses from the database. Each function gives the respective model (Student, Teacher, Course) with the help of objects.all() method to retrieve all records. The extracted data is then passed to an HTML template (student_list.html, teacher_list.html, course_list.html) with the help of the render function. These templates are used to show the data to users. This setup follows Django's Model-View-Template (MVT) architectures.</p><p><strong>Create Templates:</strong></p><p>Let's Create a template which shows the list of the student in the university, define the template in templates/college directory in the college app.</p><p><strong>Code:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;h1&gt;Student List&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    {% for student in students %}
        &lt;li&gt;{{ student.name }} - {{ student.roll_number }}&lt;/li&gt;
    {% endfor %}
&lt;/ul&gt;
</textarea></div><p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p><p>The above HTML template displays a list of students in Django's application. The &lt;h1&gt; tag offers a heading that provides the title "Student List". The &lt;ul&gt; element generates an unordered list, and the {% for a student in students %} loops iterates using the student's context variable passed from the view. Inside the loop, the name of each student and roll_number are shown as list items (&lt;li&gt;). The {% endfor %} closes the loop. This template generates the content on the basis of student data dynamically offered by the Django view.</p><p>Now, make a list to show the list of teachers.</p><p><strong>teacher_list.html</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;h1&gt;Teacher List&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    {% for teacher in teachers %}
        &lt;li&gt;{{ teacher.name }} - {{ teacher.department }}&lt;/li&gt;
    {% endfor %}
&lt;/ul&gt;
</textarea></div><p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p><p>The above HTML template provides the Django application for displaying a list of teachers. The &lt;h1&gt; shows the "Teacher List" heading. The &lt;ul&gt; element shows the unordered list; on the other hand {% for teacher in teachers %} loop iterates using the teachers variable passed from the Django view. Inside the loop, the name of each teacher and department name are shown within a list item (&lt;li&gt;). The {% endfor %} tag marks the end of the loop. This setup confirms that the list is generated dynamically on the basis of teacher data extracted from the database.</p><p><strong>Course_list.html</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;h1&gt;Course List&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    {% for course in courses %}
        &lt;li&gt;{{ course.name }} - {{ course.code }} ({{ course.teacher.name }})&lt;/li&gt;
    {% endfor %}
&lt;/ul&gt;
</textarea></div><p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p><p>This is the template used when listing courses in a Django application. It uses a &lt;h1&gt; tag and the title "Course List." It then uses an unordered list element to generate it-that is, &lt;ul&gt; as well-and will make use of the `{% for the course in courses %}` loop, which will iterate over the variable passed to it from the Django view, `courses`. The `name`, `code`, and `name` for the assigned teacher (`course.teacher.name`) are displayed in every list item as `&lt;li&gt;. The `end for` tag ends the loop. The template here is dynamically creating a list of courses based on information fetched from the database.</p><h3 class="h3">Routing of URL:</h3><p>Define the URLs in college/urls.py by creating a urls.py file in the college app and adding the following code:</p><p><strong>Code:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">from django.urls import path
from . import views

urlpatterns = [
    path('students/', views.student_list, name='student_list'),
    path('teachers/', views.teacher_list, name='teacher_list'),
    path('courses/', views.course_list, name='course_list'),
]
</textarea></div><p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p><p>This Django code defines URL patterns for a web application. In the above example, the URL patterns list maps particular URL paths to corresponding view functions in the views.py file. The path('students/', views.student_list, name='student_list') will map the /students/ URL to the student_list view. Similarly, teacher_list and course_list have similar paths. Each URL pattern can be assigned a unique identifier through the name parameter, which will make it easier to refer to them in templates and redirects. This setup follows Django's URL routing system, ensuring that HTTP requests are directed to the correct views for processing and response generation.</p><p>Also, add the college URLs in Project URLs.</p><p>Open the college_management/urls.py file and add the college app URL.</p><p><strong>Code:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">from django.contrib import admin
from django.urls import path, include

urlpatterns = [
    path('admin/', admin.site.urls),
    path('college/', include('college.urls')),
]
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Admin Interface:</h3><p>Django offers a built-in admin interface to handle the data. Register all the models in the admin.py file.</p><p>To add models, open the college/admin.py file and add all the models.</p><p><strong>Code:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">from django.contrib import admin
from .models import Student, Teacher, Course, Attendance

admin.site.register(Student)
admin.site.register(Teacher)
admin.site.register(Course)
admin.site.register(Attendance)
</textarea></div><p>After adding all the models, create a superuser to create the admin user by typing the command.</p><p><strong>Command:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">python manage.py createsuperuser
</textarea></div><p>When you run this command, you will be asked for the username, email ID, and password. After entering all the details, you will now be an admin, and you can access all the data.</p><img alt="College Management System using Django - Python Project" src="https://d2jdgazzki9vjm.cloudfront.net/django/images/college-management-system-using-django-python-project.png"/><p>Now, let's access the admin panel. To access the admin panel, run the server using the command:</p><p><strong>Command:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">python manage.py runserver
</textarea></div><p>To open the admin panel, visit https://127.0.0.1:8000/admin/ and log in with the superuser's credentials.</p><img alt="College Management System using Django - Python Project" src="https://d2jdgazzki9vjm.cloudfront.net/django/images/college-management-system-using-django-python-project2.png"/><p>In the above image, you can see the Django administration panel, which shows the users and groups and the college panel, which shows the attendances, list of courses, list of students, and list of teachers. You can change, update, modify and delete the data in the admin panel.</p><p>Now, our college management system is ready, and some data is added using the admin panel to check whether the website is functional or not. To test the views, visit the following urls.</p><ul class="points"><li>http://127.0.0.1:8000/college/students/</li><li>http://127.0.0.1:8000/college/teachers/</li><li>http://127.0.0.1:8000/college/courses/</li></ul><hr/><div class="nexttopicdiv"><span class="nexttopictext">Next Topic</span><span class="nexttopiclink"><a href="create-a-word-counter-app-using-django">Create-a-word-counter-app-using-django</a></span></div><br/> <div id="bottomnext"><a class="next" href="php-vs-django" style="float:left">← prev</a> <a class="next" href="create-a-word-counter-app-using-django" style="float:right">next →</a></div><br/> </td></tr></tbody></table></div></body></html><br/></div><div class="related-posts-container"><h2 class="h3">Related Posts</h2><ul class="related-posts-list"><li><a class="related-post-item" href="php-vs-django"><h3 class="h3">PHP vs. Django</h3><p class="related-post-content">| Important Difference between PHP and Django In this tutorial, we will describe the difference between PHP and Django framework. We will make a detailed comparison between these two leading web development technologies. Django is a framework, and PHP is a web development language. Django guides...</p><p class="reading-time"><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa 